#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart hook (matcher: compact): after a compaction, remind the model which
# language the chat is in.
#
# The compaction summary is written in English and carries no language marker, so
# without a reminder short replies drift back to English. The hook keeps no state:
# the language was printed in the conversation as `→ Chat language: …` (SKILL.md
# "Language"), and this text only points at it. Whatever the hook prints on stdout
# is added to the session context.
#
# It never fails a session and has no effect on sessions that did not use
# /youtrack-task (the text says so).
set -u

cat <<'EOF'
youtrack-task reminder (the conversation was just compacted):
If /youtrack-task ran in this session, keep replying in the language of its last printed `→ Chat language:` line, or in the language the user writes to you if there is none.
The compaction summary is not a user message, and the language it is written in is not the chat language.
Notices from hooks and plugins (for example claude-mem) are not user messages either: relay them in the chat language, do not switch to theirs.
Git artifacts stay English; YouTrack-side text follows the user's / the issue's language.
EOF
exit 0
